Below is a **complete, minimal example** that shows how to add **MapLibre GL JS** to a **Vite + TypeScript** project when you install the library from npm (no CDN).  
It works with plain vanilla TypeScript, but the same ideas apply if you’re using a framework (React, Vue, Svelte, …).

---

## 1️⃣  Install the npm packages

```bash
# From the root of your Vite project
npm i maplibre-gl
# (optional) If you want the official MapLibre type definitions – they are already bundled,
# but the @types package can be useful for older editors.
npm i -D @types/maplibre-gl
```

> **Why not a CDN?**  
> Installing from npm lets you get proper TypeScript typings, tree‑shaking, and easy version control.

---

## 2️⃣  Project structure (one possible layout)

```
my-vite-app/
├─ public/
│   └─ index.html
├─ src/
│   ├─ main.ts          ← entry point (Vite loads this)
│   └─ map.ts           ← map‑initialisation helper (optional)
├─ vite.config.ts
├─ tsconfig.json
└─ package.json
```

---

## 3️⃣  `index.html`

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <title>MapLibre GL + Vite + TS</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
  <!-- Vite injects the compiled script automatically -->
</head>
<body>
  <!-- The map will be rendered into this div -->
  <div id="map" style="width: 100vw; height: 100vh;"></div>
</body>
</html>
```

> **Tip:** The `style` attribute above makes the map fill the whole viewport.  
> You can also move the CSS to a separate file if you prefer.

---

## 4️⃣  `src/main.ts`

```ts
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Import the library + its CSS (the CSS is required for
//     controls, sprites, fonts, etc.)
// ------------------------------------------------------------
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Optional: Pull a style URL from an environment variable.
//     Vite exposes variables that start with VITE_ via
//     import.meta.env.  This is handy for swapping between
//     dev / prod styles without code changes.
// ------------------------------------------------------------
const MAP_STYLE = import.meta.env.VITE_MAP_STYLE as string
  ?? 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json'; // fallback demo style

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Initialise the map once the DOM is ready.
// ------------------------------------------------------------
function initMap() {
  // The container must exist in the DOM (the <div id="map"> above)
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',          // id of the container element
    style: MAP_STYLE,          // style URL or JSON object
    center: [0, 0],            // [lng, lat] – start position
    zoom: 2,                   // initial zoom
    // optional: you can set maxZoom, minZoom, pitch, bearing, etc.
  });

  // --------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  Add a navigation control (zoom + compass) – optional
  // --------------------------------------------------------
  map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());

  // --------------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣  Example: add a marker at the centre
  // --------------------------------------------------------
  new maplibregl.Marker()
    .setLngLat([0, 0])
    .addTo(map);
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 6️⃣  Wait for the DOM to be ready (Vite bundles this as a
//     module, so the script runs after the HTML is parsed,
//     but we still guard against race conditions)
// ------------------------------------------------------------
if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', initMap);
} else {
  initMap();
}
```

### What’s happening?

| Line(s) | Explanation |
|--------|--------------|
| `import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';` | Pulls the library’s CSS into the Vite bundle (Vite’s CSS handling will inject it into the page). |
| `import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';` | The default export is the `Map` constructor and all the helper classes. |
| `import.meta.env.VITE_MAP_STYLE` | Vite’s way to read environment variables at **build time**. You can set it in a `.env` file (see below). |
| `new maplibregl.Map({ … })` | Creates the map. The only required options are `container`, `style`, `center`, and `zoom`. |
| `map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());` | Adds the familiar zoom‑in/zoom‑out + compass UI. |
| `new maplibregl.Marker()` | Shows how to add a simple marker. |

---

## 5️⃣  (Optional) Environment variables for the style URL

Create a file called **`.env`** in the project root:

```dotenv
# .env
VITE_MAP_STYLE=https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json
# Or, for a MapTiler style (requires an API key):
# VITE_MAP_STYLE=https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY
```

> **Why use `VITE_`?**  
> Vite only exposes variables that start with `VITE_` to the client bundle. Anything else stays private to the build process.

You can also create `.env.production` and `.env.development` to have different styles per environment.

---

## 6️⃣  Vite config (no special changes needed)

A vanilla Vite config works out‑of‑the‑box:

```ts
// vite.config.ts
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import { resolve } from 'node:path';

export default defineConfig({
  // If you want to serve the app from a sub‑folder, set base:
  // base: '/my-app/',
  resolve: {
    alias: {
      // Optional: shorten imports, e.g. '@/utils' → src/utils
      '@': resolve(__dirname, 'src'),
    },
  },
});
```

> **Note:** MapLibre GL does **not** need a Web Worker configuration (unlike Mapbox GL JS v2). The library ships a tiny worker internally, and Vite will bundle it automatically.

---

## 7️⃣  Run / Build

```bash
# Development server (hot‑module replacement)
npm run dev

# Production build
npm run build
```

You should see a full‑screen map rendered with the demo style (or whatever style you configured).

---

## 8️⃣  Quick checklist for a production‑ready setup

| ✅ | Item | Why it matters |
|---|------|----------------|
| 1 | **Lock the MapLibre version** (`"maplibre-gl": "^4.2.0"` or whatever you need) | Guarantees reproducible builds. |
| 2 | **Pin a style URL** (self‑hosted style JSON or a stable third‑party service) | Avoids breaking changes if the demo style disappears. |
| 3 | **Set `crossOrigin: "anonymous"`** on the map if you need to export the canvas (e.g., screenshots). |
| 4 | **Add a fallback UI** for browsers without WebGL (`maplibregl.supported()` check). |
| 5 | **Configure CSP** (Content‑Security‑Policy) to allow `style-src` and `script-src` for the map tiles provider. |
| 6 | **Cache tiles** (via service worker or HTTP cache headers) for better performance. |
| 7 | **Consider a custom sprite/font** if you want a brand‑consistent look. |

---

## 9️⃣  TL;DR – All the code in one place

```bash
# 1️⃣ Install
npm i maplibre-gl
npm i -D @types/maplibre-gl   # optional
```

**`.env`**

```dotenv
VITE_MAP_STYLE=https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json
```

**`public/index.html`**

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <title>MapLibre GL + Vite + TS</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
</head>
<body>
  <div id="map" style="width:100vw;height:100vh;"></div>
</body>
</html>
```

**`src/main.ts`**

```ts
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

const MAP_STYLE = import.meta.env.VITE_MAP_STYLE as string
  ?? 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json';

function initMap() {
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: MAP_STYLE,
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
  });

  map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());

  new maplibregl.Marker()
    .setLngLat([0, 0])
    .addTo(map);
}

if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', initMap);
} else {
  initMap();
}
```

**`vite.config.ts`** (optional)

```ts
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import { resolve } from 'node:path';

export default defineConfig({
  resolve: {
    alias: { '@': resolve(__dirname, 'src') },
  },
});
```

Run `npm run dev` → **MapLibre GL** appears, fully typed, bundled by Vite, and ready for production.

---

### 🎉 You’re all set!

Feel free to extend this skeleton:

* Load GeoJSON sources (`map.addSource` / `map.addLayer`).
* Use the **MapLibre GL Draw** plugin for interactive editing.
* Integrate with a UI framework (React, Vue, Svelte) – just move the `initMap` call into a component’s lifecycle hook (`useEffect`, `onMounted`, …).

If you hit any snags (CORS, style JSON errors, etc.) just drop a follow‑up question—happy mapping! 🚀
